---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CowTree from "../../components/CowTree.astro";
import MirrorHeal from "../../components/MirrorHeal.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1): the question, a plain answer,
// the figures, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("copy-on-write");
const L = {
  passRewrite: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 128),
  hole: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 4345, 4346),
  writeDone: gh("module/zfs/dbuf.c", 5152, 5154),
  dvas: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 362),
  label: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 175, 177),
  fletcherDefault: gh("include/sys/zio.h", 119, 120),
  compare: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 535, 536),
  counter: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 5295, 5300),
  repairCond: gh("module/zfs/vdev_mirror.c", 883, 886),
  noGood: gh("module/zfs/vdev_mirror.c", 878, 881),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="copy-on-write"
  title="What happens when I change a block? Copy-on-write, checksums and self-healing · Uberblok"
  description="ZFS writes a changed block to a new place and keeps its checksum in the parent, so a crash cannot leave a half-written pool and wrong data from a disk is caught on read. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>What happens when I change a block?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          Two things can go wrong under a filesystem. The power can fail in the middle of a write and leave a file
          part old and part new. And a disk can return wrong data without reporting an error. A bit can flip. A write
          can be lost, or land in the wrong place.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS never writes over a block that is in use. The new version of a block goes to free space, and the old
          one stays where it was. The blocks that point to it are copied the same way, up to the top of the pool. Then one
          small write, to a root record called the uberblock, switches the pool to the new version. If the power fails
          before that write, the pool comes back at the last finished version, with nothing half written.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each pointer also stores a checksum of the block it points to, and every block read from disk is checked
          against it. Wrong data gets caught. If the pool has redundancy, such as a mirror, ZFS takes the good data
          from another copy and writes it over the bad one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="never">
      <h2 id="never"><span class="n">1</span> The new version goes somewhere else</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Pick a data block in the figure and step through the change. The new block gets a new address. Its parent
          holds the pointer to it, so the parent changes as well and moves to a new address. The same happens to the
          top of the file’s tree.
        </p>
        <p>
          Until the last step, the pool still points to the old tree, and nothing in that tree has been touched. The
          last step writes the uberblock, and only then does the pool use the new tree. Cut the power before that
          step to see what the pool finds when it comes back. Take a snapshot first, and the old blocks stay on disk.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CowTree lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="parent">
      <h2 id="parent"><span class="n">2</span> The parent holds the checksum</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A checksum kept next to the block it protects misses some faults. Say a write never reaches the disk. The
          place it was meant for still holds older data, and that data still matches the checksum stored beside it.
          The read looks fine and returns the wrong thing.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS keeps the checksum in the parent, inside the pointer. The parent is checked by its own parent, and so on
          up to the uberblock. When a block is read from disk, ZFS computes its checksum again and compares it with the one in
          the pointer. Damage one side of the mirror below, then read the file. ZFS catches the bad copy, reads the
          other side, and writes the good data back over the bad copy.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MirrorHeal lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figures follow four places in the OpenZFS source. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from release
        zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Writing a block</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>zio_write_compress</code> decides whether a write may go over the old block. It may only when the
          block was created in the current transaction group (txg), the batch of changes ZFS writes out together every
          few seconds. No committed uberblock points to such a block yet. Otherwise the pointer is cleared, and the
          block normally gets a new address.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_write_compress</code>, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/zio.c"
            from={2094}
            to={2105}
            symbol="zio_write_compress"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 2094,
                to: 2101,
                text: `Writing over the old block is allowed only for a block born in the transaction group being synced (<code>BP_GET_BIRTH(bp) == zio-&gt;io_txg</code>), of the same size, from sync pass 2 on (<a href="${L.passRewrite}"><code>zfs_sync_pass_rewrite</code></a>). No committed uberblock points to such a block yet.`,
              },
              {
                from: 2102,
                to: 2104,
                text: `Every other write clears the pointer and takes the full write pipeline. <code>zio_dva_allocate</code> <a href="${L.hole}">checks that the pointer is empty</a> before it takes a new address. When the write is done, <a href="${L.writeDone}"><code>dbuf_write_done</code></a> frees the old block, or leaves it to a snapshot that still uses it.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Storing the checksum</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A block pointer, <code>blkptr_t</code>, takes 128 bytes. It holds up to three addresses, for copies of the
          same block, and one 256-bit checksum. The copies hold the same bytes, so the one checksum checks any of them.
          The pointer sits inside the parent block, so the parent checks the child.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>blkptr_t</code>, spa.h</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="include/sys/spa.h"
            from={374}
            to={382}
            symbol="blkptr_t"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 375,
                text: `Up to three addresses for the same block (<a href="${L.dvas}"><code>SPA_DVAS_PER_BP</code></a>), all covered by the one checksum.`,
              },
              {
                from: 381,
                text: `The checksum of the block this pointer leads to. The uberblock has no parent, so it carries <a href="${L.label}">its own checksum</a> instead.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Reading a block</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          On every read from disk, <code>zio_checksum_error_impl</code> takes the expected checksum from the pointer
          the read came through. It computes the actual checksum over the bytes the disk returned. If the two differ,
          that copy is rejected with <code>ECKSUM</code>, and the error counts against that disk in the CKSUM column
          of <code>zpool status</code>.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_checksum_error_impl</code>, zio_checksum.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/zio_checksum.c"
            from={501}
            to={506}
            symbol="zio_checksum_error_impl"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 503,
                text: "The expected value comes from the block pointer the reader followed, in the parent.",
              },
              {
                from: 504,
                to: 505,
                text: `The actual value is computed over the bytes just read. With <code>checksum=on</code>, the default, the function is <a href="${L.fletcherDefault}">fletcher4</a>. The two are <a href="${L.compare}">compared further down</a>, and each mismatch is <a href="${L.counter}">counted on the disk</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Repairing a mirror</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          When one side of a mirror fails the check, ZFS reads another side. If that copy is good and the pool is
          writable, it writes the good data to each side that failed. If no side is good, the read returns an error,
          and the application never gets the wrong bytes.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_mirror_io_done</code>, vdev_mirror.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/vdev_mirror.c"
            from={927}
            to={933}
            symbol="vdev_mirror_io_done"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 927,
                to: 933,
                text: `The good buffer, <code>zio-&gt;io_abd</code>, is written to each damaged side as a repair write, marked <code>ZIO_FLAG_SELF_HEAL</code> when the cause was an unexpected error. This runs only with a good copy and a writable pool (<a href="${L.repairCond}">the condition</a>). With no good copy, <a href="${L.noGood}">the read fails</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figures leave out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figures show one file, so the tree is short. In a real pool it runs from the uberblock through the MOS
        (the pool’s own metadata), the dataset and the file’s dnode. They leave out sync passes, gang blocks, dedup,
        nopwrite and encryption. Writes an application forced to disk with fsync also go to the ZIL, the intent log,
        and are replayed after a crash. The ZIL room shows that. The mirror here always reads disk A first. ZFS picks the side
        by load. The uberblock ring has 32 slots, as on disks with 4K sectors. The checksums are real fletcher4 values
        over the demo blocks. Addresses and txg numbers are made up.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="copy-on-write" />
</Base>

